  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "Resolve as faithful-equivalent under the recorded user-adopted interpretation. The disagreement concerns missing measure evidence in the blind translation, not incompatible interpretations of the remaining target. The native supplement supplies the missing identification; the decision follows from its declarations and the target definitions, not agreement between judges.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target contains unrestricted geometric transport and two analytic-domain biconditionals. The recorded convention specifies precisely these domains. The supplement's fully explicit Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id identifies the target's exact MeasureSpace.volume Real Real.measureSpace with StieltjesFunction.id.measure."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The requested measure investigation is resolved. The selected measure is ordinary real length measure, with its normalization established independently of declaration names. No separate dependency-reuse interpretation is needed.",
      "primary_evidence": "Native output expands Real.measureSpace to measureSpaceOfInnerProductSpace on Real and then to an orthonormal-basis normalized additive Haar measure. Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id identifies that measure with the identity Stieltjes measure; Real.volume_Ioc states volume((a,b])=ENNReal.ofReal(b-a)."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "The evidence gap supporting undetermined is closed for this interpreted comparison. Both complete implications are yes, yielding faithful-equivalent and qualified acceptance. The printed source's unresolved analytic scope remains a separate limitation.",
      "primary_evidence": "The round-trip judgment attributes its uncertainty specifically to the unidentified measure. The supplied native supplement identifies the exact measure argument appearing in D002, D025 and the elaborated target. Printed page 1 and pages 17-18 retain the distinction between unrestricted introductory wording and the later smooth formulation."
    },
    {
      "item": "blind translator left dependency_coverage item D056 unclear",
      "resolution": "D056 is understood with the supplement: its measurable space is the Borel structure on the ordinary real topology, and its distinguished volume is normalized real length measure. It is neither arbitrary nor zero nor differently scaled. Consequently the blind translation's symbolic μ can be identified with ordinary length measure in this adjudication.",
      "primary_evidence": "The supplement displays Real.measurableSpace := borel ℝ, RealMeasureDependency.selectedBorel, the explicit instance selected by Real.measureSpace, and fully explicit types of Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id and Real.volume_Ioc using @MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume Real Real.measureSpace. StieltjesFunction.id has underlying function id. These declarations establish identity and interval normalization; the supplied hashes record provenance rather than semantic correctness."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "C04 passes under the recorded interpretation. Everywhere differentiability and local interval integrability are necessary-and-sufficient analytic conditions inside the conclusion, not outer restrictions on profiles. Boundary-flux integrability adds no restriction to the adopted translated-profile class.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001 and D004 require both actual partial derivatives everywhere. D014 is everywhere differentiability. D002 requires spatial and temporal flux integrability, and D025 uses integrability on both oriented half-open intervals. For locally integrable p and nonzero c, τ↦c p(x-cτ) is locally integrable by invertible affine substitution; at c=0 it is zero. The supplement fixes ordinary length measure."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "C05 passes under the recorded interpretation. All three adopted scopes are represented: geometric transport for every profile, the exact classical domain, and the exact rectangle-conservation domain. The initial identity and characteristic derivative are consequences of translation and do not introduce another selected theorem.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 is p(x-ct). The first three target conjuncts give q(x,0)=p(x), q(x+ct,t)=p(x), and the derivative of τ↦q(x+cτ,τ) equal to zero. The last two conjuncts are the adopted domain equivalences. Printed page 1 states the translation and unchanged shape; printed page 18 explains characteristic constancy."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C06 unclear",
      "resolution": "C06 passes at the supplied semantic boundaries. The disputed measure and the operators using it now have explicit interpretations. The local predicates encode derivative existence and integrable rectangle balance, preserving the transport sign and flux orientation.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 evaluates p(x-ct); D004 existentially certifies temporal and spatial derivatives with qt+c•qx=0; D002 equates later minus earlier spatial content with integrated left minus right flux. D025 requires interval integrability, and D082 defines oriented integrals as the difference of two restricted integrals. D029 projects the measure identified by the native supplement. The explicit real instances retain ordinary arithmetic, scalar multiplication and topology."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "C10 passes under the recorded interpretation. The integral profile class is exactly local interval integrability for ordinary length measure. The theorem preserves all real profiles geometrically, all real speeds, and the full real space-time domain; it imposes no finite-mass, boundedness, positivity or support restriction.",
      "primary_evidence": "The elaborated target universally quantifies profile:Real→Real and speed:Real, with all coordinates and endpoints real. Its final condition is ∀ a b, IntervalIntegrable profile volume a b. Printed page 1 supplies the inherited real space-time setting. D025 and the supplement identify the intended local integral class."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "N06 passes under the recorded interpretation. The equivalences characterize solutionhood of the explicitly translated field; they do not classify arbitrary solutions or assert uniqueness. Both analytic classes are nonempty and substantively distinct.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 fixes the field throughout the target. In D004, spatial differentiation at t=0 recovers profile differentiability, even at zero speed. In D002, spatial integrability at t=0 recovers profile interval integrability. A nonconstant polynomial satisfies both analytic classes; a step profile satisfies the integral class but not the differentiability class. Printed page 18's classification and initial-value discussion are contextual, not additional selected conclusions."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "This implication is yes under the recorded user-adopted interpretation after identifying μ with ordinary length measure. The reconstructed five conjuncts recover every part of the interpreted source assertion.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind translation explicitly reconstructs D001-D004 and all five conjuncts. Its sole listed ambiguity concerns D056. The native supplement identifies the exact measure used by the target, while printed equations (1.2)-(1.3) fix the PDE, translation and propagation direction."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "This implication is yes under the recorded user-adopted interpretation. The adopted domains supply the two biconditionals, and the translation formula supplies the remaining identities. The zero derivative is a derivative of a constant characteristic restriction, without assuming derivatives of an arbitrary profile.",
      "primary_evidence": "Substitution in printed (1.3) gives p(x-c·0)=p(x) and p((x+cτ)-cτ)=p(x). D022 expresses an actual derivative of this constant composite. The recorded convention supplies exact differentiability and rectangle-integrability domains; D001-D004, D025, D082 and the native measure identification express those domains."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, unfolding D003 gives exactly q(x,t)=p(x-ct). The unrestricted characteristic equality gives constant-speed, unchanged-shape transport for every real profile and speed. The fourth conjunct, through D001 and D004, gives classical solutionhood with both partial derivatives exactly when p is everywhere differentiable. The fifth conjunct, through D002, gives finite-rectangle balance with spatial and boundary-flux integrability exactly when p is locally interval integrable. The native supplement identifies every volume argument as ordinary real length measure. Thus the complete interpreted claim follows, including zero and negative speeds. This is not an implication to an unrestricted classical-solution claim for every profile, which the source context does not support."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, the displayed translated formula entails initial agreement and characteristic constancy; the characteristic composite therefore has derivative zero without profile regularity. The adopted classical characterization matches D001/D004: a differentiable profile gives partial derivatives -c p'(x-ct) and p'(x-ct), satisfying the PDE, while the spatial section at time zero forces profile differentiability in the reverse direction. The adopted rectangle characterization matches D002 with the identified length measure. Translation and invertible affine substitution give the required slice integrability and integrated balance for locally integrable profiles when c is nonzero; at zero speed spatial content is stationary and flux is zero. Conversely spatial integrability at time zero recovers the profile condition. Oriented integrals handle reversed endpoints by sign and equal endpoints by zero. These are the target's complete five conjuncts, with no extra uniqueness or boundary-value conclusion."
    }
  },
  "classification": "faithful-equivalent",
  "accepted": true,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The pinned PDF does not itself explicitly specify this complete analytic profile class. Its introductory arbitrary-profile wording and nonsmooth solution sense remain unresolved without the recorded user-adopted interpretation; this acceptance does not settle a source-only comparison.",
    "Printed equation (11.33) has the displayed spatial-bound inconsistency in its initial-data term. That contextual issue remains unresolved and is not silently corrected here; the target uses the separately adopted finite-rectangle formulation."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "resolved-measure-evidence-gap",
      "evidence": "The blind dossier exposes D056 only as inferInstance. The native supplement expands that instance and gives fully explicit Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id and Real.volume_Ioc declarations for the same volume argument used by the target.",
      "impact": "The round-trip judge's material measure uncertainty is resolved by declaration evidence, without inferring meaning from a name or accepting a hash as proof of correspondence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-acceptance",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 gives arbitrary-profile translation; pages 4-5 distinguish discontinuous and classical solutions; pages 17-18 discuss smooth profiles. The separate recorded convention explicitly fixes the exact differentiability and local-integrability domains.",
      "impact": "Faithful-equivalent and both yes implications apply only under the recorded user-adopted interpretation for this selected row. They do not attribute these exact analytic domains to the printed book."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "nonvacuity-and-strength",
      "evidence": "The target has no outer regularity premises. D004 requires actual spatial derivatives even at zero speed; D002 retains spatial integrability even at zero flux. Volume((0,1])=1. Polynomial, step and profiles singular like 1/|x| near zero distinguish the analytic cases while all retain geometric transport.",
      "impact": "There is no reduced applicability disguised as strength, no zero-measure trivialization and no reliance on totalized integrals to certify nonintegrable profiles. The additional geometric conjuncts are consequences of translation, so faithful-equivalent is appropriate rather than faithful-stronger."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached primary-source images, exact target types, supplied dependency declarations, blind reconstruction, both judgments and native supplement resolves every trigger. The measure supplement establishes the exact missing identification and normalization, which closes the downstream checklist and implication uncertainties. The target preserves scalar roles, quantification, real domains, signs, characteristic transport and both adopted analytic classes. Acceptance is therefore faithful-equivalent under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, with the original source-only limitations retained. No tools were used, no files were accessed or written, and no supplied source, rendering, runtime or dossier hashes were independently recomputed. The decision uses statement and definition evidence, not target proof evidence."
}
